{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"### The strategy\n\nWe can first rewrite each rational term of $f$ as a negative power of $x$.\n\nThen, the derivatives of these terms can be found using **the power rule**:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=n\\cdot x^{n-1}$\n\n(Remember that this applies even when $n$ is negative.)","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Rewriting rational terms as negative powers\n\n$\\begin{align}\nf(x)\u0026=2x^2-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}+\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=2x^2-x^{-2}+x^{-3}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Differentiating using the power rule\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}f'(x)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(2x^2-x^{-2}+x^{-3})\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=2\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^2)-\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-2})+\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-3})\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=2( 2x)-(-2)x^{-3}+(-3)x^{-4}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4x+\\dfrac{2}{x^3}-\\dfrac{3}{x^4}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $f'(x)=4x+\\dfrac{2}{x^3}-\\dfrac{3}{x^4}$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $f(x)=2x^2-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}+\\dfrac{1}{x^3}$ .\n\n$f'(x)=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"4x+\\frac{2}{x^3}-\\frac{3}{x^4}"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
